WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.440
A genome browser provides a way for us to&nbsp;
view a genome sequence along with various

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.880
pieces of information or features that&nbsp;
have been identified in that sequence.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.560
You are probably most familiar with the&nbsp;
term "browser" in the context of an internet

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.080
browser, such as Safari, Chrome, or Firefox,&nbsp;
that provides a way for us to access and

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:34.160
search for websites on the internet. The Genome&nbsp;
Browser works similarly for genome sequences.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.920
The Genome Browser we are using allows us&nbsp;
to view the genome assemblies of Drosophila,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.560
or fruit fly species, although our work for these&nbsp;
modules will be focused on Drosophila melanogaster,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.920
the most commonly used species in the&nbsp;
laboratory. Let's take a look at the various

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.200
things we can view using a genome browser.&nbsp;
Navigate to the Genome Browser by typing

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:05.520
gander.wustl.edu and clicking on the Genome&nbsp;
Browser link on the left side of the page.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.800
This will take you to the gateway page. At the&nbsp;
gateway page, let's click to reset the browser

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.440
user interface settings to their defaults. After we&nbsp;
do this, we need to choose which genome we want to

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:28.000
investigate. Choose the D. melanogaster genome and&nbsp;
the July 2014 gene assembly. We will be looking

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.520
at contig1. After we hit submit, we are brought to&nbsp;
the view of this region. At the top of this page, we

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.400
see the name of the species and the assembly, and&nbsp;
the position should show contig1:1-

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:49.520
11 000. This means that we are viewing 11 000&nbsp;
base pairs of the Drosophila melanogaster genome.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.960
We can see the base pair numbers along the row&nbsp;
called "scale." Let's first hit the "hide all" button,

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.520
so we can choose which features of the genome&nbsp;
we want to view. We use the term "features" in

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:10.240
bioinformatics as a catch-all term for anything&nbsp;
associated with the genome. Each of these features

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.320
can be highlighted or displayed along with the&nbsp;
genome sequence as a track that can be viewed in

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:23.840
the browser. We can turn on and turn off various&nbsp;
tracks and also adjust how each is displayed.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.160
Let's look at a few features. Start&nbsp;
by clicking on the Base Position link

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.400
under Mapping and Sequencing Tracks. By clicking on&nbsp;
this, we can view a description of this particular

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.280
track and change its settings if we wish. As the&nbsp;
description states, the Base Position feature in

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:49.280
the Genome Browser annotations track image shows&nbsp;
the genomic coordinates of the displayed region

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:56.240
and when zoomed into the base level, you can see&nbsp;
the nucleotides within that region. The description

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:02.800
also tells us more about how to view the&nbsp;
individual bases. Let's go back to the browser by

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:10.640
clicking the back button and view this track. Right&nbsp;
now, the base position is set at "dense." We can't see

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:17.200
individual bases because we are too zoomed out.&nbsp;
Let's zoom in to the first 100 base pairs of this

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:24.400
genomic sequence by typing contig1:1-100 into the position/search

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:32.880
box. After we hit return, we are now able to see the&nbsp;
individual bases of one strand of the DNA sequence

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:39.840
A's, T's, C's, and G's. If you click on the arrow&nbsp;
below the scale contig1 label, you can see the

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:47.200
strand that is complementary to the top strand., in&nbsp;
other words, the bottom strand. Click the arrow one

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:53.840
more time to view the top strand again. Let's&nbsp;
change the base position track to "full" to see

00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:00.800
more information associated with this feature.&nbsp;
Click "full" and hit "refresh." Below the bases, we

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:08.080
now see gray boxes. These indicate the amino acids&nbsp;
that are called for by each codon, or set of three

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.560
bases. There are three rows of these boxes because&nbsp;
there are three possible reading frames, depending

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:21.600
on which base is used to begin the first codon, in&nbsp;
other words, where the reading frame is established.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.120
So the first reading frame at the top starts&nbsp;
with the very first base of the sequence.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.640
The second reading frame starts with the second&nbsp;
base, and the third frame with the third base.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.440
For each strand of DNA there are&nbsp;
three possible reading frames,

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.920
so there are a total of six possible reading&nbsp;
frames because DNA is double stranded.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.360
Some boxes are labeled green; these are for&nbsp;
the amino acid methionine. Translation always

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.520
starts at a codon that codes for methionine,&nbsp;
which almost always has the sequence A-T-G.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:05.760
The red boxes indicate stop codons. For&nbsp;
example, T-A-A calls for an end to translation.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:12.880
Let's look at another feature: the genes that are&nbsp;
located in this region of the genome. Let's zoom

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:21.600
out to view the entire region by typing in contig1:1-11 000 in the position/search

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:28.800
box. Notice that we no longer see the bases or&nbsp;
amino acids. There is not enough room to draw

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:36.560
all the nucleotides and amino acids. Let's turn&nbsp;
on the FlyBase Gene track to "pack." This shows us

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:43.120
the location of genes as indicated by boxes and&nbsp;
lines. The names of the genes are located to the

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:49.120
left of each graphical representation of the gene&nbsp;
with the gene name shown to the left of the hyphen.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:57.920
This shows us that there are three genes&nbsp;
in this region of the genome: CG32165,  spd-2,

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:05.840
and tra. For these modules, we will be spending&nbsp;
most of our time learning about the tra gene.

